WEBVTT

1
00:00:02.700 --> 00:00:03.706
The gravy burns.

2
00:00:03.706 --> 00:00:08.295
Nobody in the kitchen has to be told: the smell reaches everyone at once.

3
00:00:08.295 --> 00:00:16.907
The cook takes the potatoes off the heat too, because the main course can't go out without gravy, and she tells the family what happened.

4
00:00:17.133 --> 00:00:23.197
But by the window, three trays for tomorrow's bake sale are in the oven: cookies, scones and muffins.

5
00:00:23.197 --> 00:00:25.538
They have nothing to do with the gravy.

6
00:00:25.538 --> 00:00:28.780
If the scones burn, the cookies should still come out.

7
00:00:29.700 --> 00:00:32.876
So here's a question that's easy to be shy about.

8
00:00:32.876 --> 00:00:36.376
An async hands back a Deferred, a pot with the lid on.

9
00:00:36.376 --> 00:00:41.496
If it fails, doesn't the failure wait under the lid until somebody calls await?

10
00:00:42.200 --> 00:00:43.116
Let's try it.

11
00:00:43.116 --> 00:00:48.470
Inside coroutineScope, the gravy is an async that burns at 100 milliseconds.

12
00:00:48.470 --> 00:00:52.134
The potatoes are a launch that needs a whole second.

13
00:00:52.134 --> 00:00:56.572
And the body waits 500 milliseconds before it awaits the gravy.

14
00:00:56.572 --> 00:00:58.404
When does anything happen?

15
00:01:01.400 --> 00:01:03.109
At 100 milliseconds.

16
00:01:03.109 --> 00:01:14.134
On a JVM that had just started, the lesson's run printed potatoes, finally, at 161, and the caller caught the burnt gravy at 188.

17
00:01:14.134 --> 00:01:17.296
The body's line at 500 never printed.

18
00:01:17.296 --> 00:01:19.176
Nobody lifted the lid.

19
00:01:19.400 --> 00:01:24.620
A failed child tells its parent the moment it fails, from async just as from launch.

20
00:01:24.620 --> 00:01:30.150
The parent cancels its other children, waits for them, and fails with the same exception.

21
00:01:30.150 --> 00:01:35.121
So a try around await comes too late: by then, everything around it has stopped.

22
00:01:35.933 --> 00:01:39.101
Now the trays, under the same kind of parent.

23
00:01:39.101 --> 00:01:44.591
The scones burn at 50 milliseconds, the muffins need 100, and the cookies 300.

24
00:01:44.591 --> 00:01:46.492
What comes out of the oven?

25
00:01:49.467 --> 00:01:50.113
Nothing.

26
00:01:50.113 --> 00:01:57.786
The cookies came off at 100, the muffins at 125, and the caller caught the burnt scones at 131.

27
00:01:57.786 --> 00:02:01.583
One burnt tray, and the whole oven was emptied.

28
00:02:01.800 --> 00:02:03.207
That's not a bake sale.

29
00:02:03.207 --> 00:02:04.431
That's a fire drill.

30
00:02:04.431 --> 00:02:07.490
For a course that needs every dish, this is right.

31
00:02:07.490 --> 00:02:10.488
For trays that don't need each other, it's wrong.

32
00:02:10.700 --> 00:02:15.584
So we change one word: supervisorScope instead of coroutineScope.

33
00:02:15.584 --> 00:02:17.839
Same trays, same burnt scones.

34
00:02:18.067 --> 00:02:21.237
This time the failure stays with the scones.

35
00:02:21.237 --> 00:02:28.155
The muffins were baked at 148 milliseconds, the cookies at 342, and the scope returned normally.

36
00:02:28.155 --> 00:02:32.551
Every line ran about 50 milliseconds late, on a busy machine.

37
00:02:32.767 --> 00:02:37.320
This parent is a supervisor: a job whose children fail on their own.

38
00:02:37.320 --> 00:02:41.673
A child's failure doesn't cancel its siblings, or the supervisor.

39
00:02:41.673 --> 00:02:46.696
But a cancel still flows down: cancel the supervisor, and every tray stops.

40
00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:49.303
So where did the scones' exception go?

41
00:02:49.303 --> 00:02:55.057
A launch has no await, so it went to the notebook by the oven, a CoroutineExceptionHandler.

42
00:02:55.057 --> 00:03:00.874
It's an element in the coroutine's context, and it writes down failures nobody else handled.

43
00:03:00.874 --> 00:03:05.743
It can't save this week's scones: by the time it runs, the coroutine is over.

44
00:03:05.967 --> 00:03:07.960
And it's only read at the top.

45
00:03:07.960 --> 00:03:14.274
An ordinary child hands its exception to its parent, so a handler on that child is never asked.

46
00:03:14.274 --> 00:03:20.920
Only root coroutines, the ones with no parent coroutine, and direct children of a supervisor use it.

47
00:03:21.733 --> 00:03:25.250
With async children, the lid finally keeps its promise.

48
00:03:25.250 --> 00:03:32.157
Under a supervisor, each failure waits in its own Deferred, and await rethrows it, where a try can catch it.

49
00:03:32.157 --> 00:03:35.802
That's how bakeAll turns every tray into baked or failed.

50
00:03:35.802 --> 00:03:41.173
Java's futures work like this without being asked: each one handles its own failure.

51
00:03:41.400 --> 00:03:47.832
For background work, the owning class builds its scope with a SupervisorJob, a dispatcher and a handler.

52
00:03:47.832 --> 00:03:49.193
That's the lab's Oven.

53
00:03:49.193 --> 00:03:53.708
Every tray launched into it fails alone, and the notebook writes it down.

54
00:03:54.433 --> 00:03:58.573
One wrong fix looks almost the same: launch(SupervisorJob()).

55
00:03:58.573 --> 00:04:00.813
It reads like a supervised child.

56
00:04:00.813 --> 00:04:09.840
But the context you pass a builder replaces the parent, so the child's parent is now a fresh SupervisorJob with no parent of its own.

57
00:04:09.840 --> 00:04:11.740
The child has left the tree.

58
00:04:11.967 --> 00:04:15.592
The lesson cancelled the dinner at 100 milliseconds.

59
00:04:15.592 --> 00:04:20.263
At 324, the trap child printed: still running, nobody cancelled me.

60
00:04:20.263 --> 00:04:22.285
It slipped out the back door.

61
00:04:22.285 --> 00:04:24.586
So never pass a Job to a builder.

62
00:04:24.586 --> 00:04:26.677
Put the supervisor in a scope.

63
00:04:27.500 --> 00:04:28.119
One limit.

64
00:04:28.119 --> 00:04:32.761
A supervisor protects the children from each other, not from its own block.

65
00:04:32.761 --> 00:04:37.218
If the body of supervisorScope throws, everything under it is cancelled.

66
00:04:37.218 --> 00:04:45.575
And here the picture breaks a little: a cook decides about each burnt dish as it happens, but a scope decides once, when you create it.

67
00:04:46.400 --> 00:04:48.882
So, does a failure wait under the lid?

68
00:04:48.882 --> 00:04:53.716
Not under a plain parent: it goes up at once, from launch and async alike.

69
00:04:53.716 --> 00:04:59.725
A supervisor keeps it with the child: in the Deferred for async, in the notebook for launch.

70
00:04:59.725 --> 00:05:02.860
If siblings need each other, use coroutineScope.

71
00:05:02.860 --> 00:05:04.951
If they don't, use a supervisor.

72
00:05:05.167 --> 00:05:06.039
Quick check.

73
00:05:06.039 --> 00:05:11.344
Inside coroutineScope, a launch child has its own handler, and it throws.

74
00:05:11.344 --> 00:05:13.016
Who gets the exception?

75
00:05:16.000 --> 00:05:17.075
Not the handler.

76
00:05:17.075 --> 00:05:23.992
A child tells its parent first, so coroutineScope is cancelled, and throws the exception to its caller.
